cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Available but Not Taken: Burnt-In Annotations Carry Label Information That Ultrasound Classifiers Do Not Use"
authors:
  - family-names: "Dar"
    given-names: "Mohsin Furkh"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1756-9087"
    affiliation: "UPES, Dehradun, India"
    email: "mohsin.dar@ddn.upes.ac.in"
repository-code: "https://github.com/MohsinFurkh/Available_Not_Taken"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Clinical ultrasound archives are annotation-rich and models trained on them are
  deployed on un-annotated live scanning. We test whether classifiers exploit
  burnt-in sonographer annotations as a shortcut, find that they do not, and
  calibrate the null by planting a synthetic shortcut of known strength, showing
  the design to be roughly 1830x more sensitive than the effect it failed to find.
keywords:
  - ultrasound
  - shortcut learning
  - dataset bias
  - null result
  - sensitivity calibration
  - fetal imaging
